# fm-comms.env — what this fleet shares, and nothing this machine already knows.
#
# The installer copies this file to /etc/fm-comms.env and you fill it in there.
# /etc/fm-comms.env is NEVER committed: the configs under zenoh/ carry ${...}
# placeholders and the installer renders them against this file. A real hostname
# or tailnet address in a tracked file is a bug.
#
# Per-host facts are NOT here. The rig's name, its ROS namespace, and the
# workspace its recordings sit under all come from this machine's identity card —
# /etc/fm/machine.json on Linux, ~/.config/fm/machine.json on macOS — which
# `fm machine init` writes. A namespace typed here as well as there disagrees the
# moment a rig is renamed, and the disagreement is invisible until a topic lands
# under a prefix nobody is subscribed to. See what this host resolves with:
#
#     ./run.sh render show
#
# Both units read this file as their EnvironmentFile, so a change here plus a
# `systemctl restart fm-zenohd` (or fm-zenoh-bridge) is the whole update path.

# --- router (the always-on office Mac mini) ----------------------------------

# Port zenohd listens on. 7447 is the Zenoh convention; keep it unless it clashes.
FM_ROUTER_PORT=7447

# The router binds TWO sockets: its LAN address and its tailnet address. Neither
# is written here — both are read off the host at render time, because both are
# per-host facts and this file holds only what the whole fleet shares. In the
# office a rig connects over the LAN; off-site, or on a Wi-Fi link that filters
# multicast, the same rig connects through the tailnet.
#
# Which interface is "the LAN" is picked by name, never by guess: the default
# route's interface unless this names another. Rune carries three LAN addresses,
# so set this on any host where the default route is not the one the fleet uses.
#FM_ROUTER_LAN_IF=en0

# Force a single bind address on a host with neither a LAN nor a tailnet worth
# binding — a CI smoke, or a bench test on an isolated network. Never on a real
# router: it replaces both sockets with one.
#FM_ROUTER_BIND_IP=127.0.0.1

# Force the whole endpoint list, comma- or space-separated. Same rule: bench
# hosts only.
#FM_ROUTER_LISTEN=tcp/192.0.2.10:7447,tcp/100.64.0.1:7447

# --- bridge (every rig) ------------------------------------------------------

# Where a machine finds the router. The router binds both its LAN address and its
# tailnet address, so this is the one value that is genuinely per-machine even
# though it lives in the fleet-wide file: a rig on the office switch takes the LAN
# endpoint and keeps its camera streams off WireGuard; a rig that leaves the
# office, or a contractor's laptop, takes the tailnet one.
#   tcp/<router-lan-ip>:7447            in the office
#   tcp/<router>.<tailnet>.ts.net:7447  anywhere else
#
# A machine that moves changes this line. It does not change its transport.
FM_ROUTER_ENDPOINT=tcp/CHANGEME:7447

# Which bridge config this machine uses: recorder | processor | robot |
# robot-anvil | workstation | cockpit. Selects zenoh/bridge-<profile>.json5.
#
# Derived, not typed. It comes from the card's `workload` field, which is what
# answers the question `role` cannot — a recorder rig and a processor rig are
# both jetsons. Set it with `fm machine init --workload recorder`.
#
# `cockpit` is the Mac: it subscribes to what the fleet publishes and publishes
# teleop commands, the mirror image of a rig's bridge.
#
# Uncomment only to force a profile for a bench run; a value set here as well as
# on the card is exactly the drift this file's split exists to avoid.
#FM_BRIDGE_PROFILE=recorder

# The DDS domain the rig's ROS nodes run on — one domain for the whole fleet. The
# installer keeps the two lines below equal: the bridge unit exports ROS_DOMAIN_ID
# from here, and the rendered config takes the domain from FM_ROS_DOMAIN_ID, so
# they cannot drift apart.
FM_ROS_DOMAIN_ID=0
ROS_DOMAIN_ID=0

# The interface this host's ROS graph lives on, when somebody else pinned it.
#
# Leave it unset on a rig or a Mac: every node there is localhost-only, and the
# bridge's DDS config probes loopback. Set it on a robot whose vendor stack names
# an interface of its own — the Anvil workcell pins CycloneDDS to one, and a
# bridge left on stock selection picks whichever interface comes first, which on
# a machine running docker is `docker0`. The two then never discover each other
# and nothing reports an error.
#
# `fm device adopt` reads this off the robot's own vendor config, so it is not
# usually typed here.
#FM_DDS_IFACE=wlp8s0

# --- episode queryable (processor rigs only) ---------------------------------

# Largest MCAP the queryable will return in one reply (bytes; 512 MiB default). A
# query is one request and one reply, so the whole file lands in memory on both
# ends — past this it refuses and tells the caller to rsync the bag instead.
FM_EPISODES_MAX_BYTES=536870912

# The recordings directory is derived — <workspace>/recordings, from the card. The
# queryable reads it and never writes; the rsync pipeline owns what lands there.
# Uncomment only on a rig whose bags genuinely live somewhere else.
#FM_EPISODES_DIR=/srv/recordings

# --- deliberate overrides ----------------------------------------------------
#
# Anything the card provides can be forced here for a bench experiment, and the
# override wins. Leave it commented on a real rig: a value set in both places is
# exactly the drift this file's split exists to avoid.
#FM_RIG_NAMESPACE=fm_rec_01
